ity to  it  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Laughter]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  when there isn't any specific rituals or  ceremonies performed in the city people  socialize they walk around they meet old  friends and family a lot of people sit  in their compounds and they sort of  prepare food there are animals walking  around in the village freely often like  cows and goats and chickens and stuff  and so everyday people prepare food by  slaughtering a cow and then cooking it  and eating it the same day and things  like there's a very immediate kind of  feeling to it the last day of the  pilgrimage is the Saturday and this is  also the kind of main event the main day  of the pilgrimage a lot of people arrive  some days before but on the Saturday is  the day that everyone comes there that's  sort of the main event of the entire  thing and in the evening on Saturday  there is this ceremony a ritual or event  where politicians and military personnel  and generally just high-ranking  individuals from society in West Africa  come to this village to talk to the  Sheikh and so they are seated on this  platform in front of all the followers  and sheikh abdul-rahman body is praised  placed right in the middle and during  this events people first of all there is  a lot of spontaneous dhikr chants that  erupts in the audience and stuff but the  politicians and the Sheikh  they hold speeches and so they talk  about the relationship between politics  and Islam and things like that like they  spoke mostly in wolf or French so I  didn't really understand most of it so I  had to have it translated by my  informants later and what they told me  was that the Sheikh holds a speech to  all the politicians and to all the  guests that are there and he holds this  long speech about ethics and talks about  that they need to be kind to the people  they need to treat them with respect and  tolerance and kindness  and that the only reason they have  powers because of the people and so they  are