ity to  it  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Laughter]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  when there isn't any specific rituals or  ceremonies performed in the city people  socialize they walk around they meet old  friends and family a lot of people sit  in their compounds and they sort of  prepare food there are animals walking  around in the village freely often like  cows and goats and chickens and stuff  and so everyday people prepare food by  slaughtering a cow and then cooking it  and eating it the same day and things  like there's a very immediate kind of  feeling to it the last day of the  pilgrimage is the Saturday and this is  also the kind of main event the main day  of the pilgrimage a lot of people arrive  some days before but on the Saturday is  the day that everyone comes there that's  sort of the main event of the entire  thing and in the evening on Saturday  there is this ceremony a ritual or event  where politicians and military personnel  and generally just high-ranking  individuals from society in West Africa  come to this village to talk to the  Sheikh and so they are seated on this  platform in front of all the followers  and sheikh abdul-rahman body is praised  placed right in the middle and during  this events people first of all there is  a lot of spontaneous dhikr chants that  erupts in the audience and stuff but the  politicians and the Sheikh  they hold speeches and so they talk  about the relationship between politics  and Islam and things like that like they  spoke mostly in wolf or French so I  didn't really understand most of it so I  had to have it translated by my  informants later and what they told me  was that the Sheikh holds a speech to  all the politicians and to all the  guests that are there and he holds this  long speech about ethics and talks about  that they need to be kind to the people  they need to treat them with respect and  tolerance and kindness  and that the only reason they have  powers because of the people and so they  are not supposed to forget about the  people and this is a way I think or I  argue to reaffirm the relationship  between politics and religion or Islam  specifically to show that these  politicians and the politics of these  countries still adhere to those ethical  ideas of the religion which give them a  legitimacy if they show that they are  following the Sheikh and they listen to  his advice and affirm that relationship  with a religion that gives them  political legitimacy and this is a  continuation of a history in Senegal and  The Gambia that's that goes back over a  hundred years it was the same during  colonial times where the Mara boots are  Sheikh often acted as a mediator between  the colonial powers and the people and  even after independence the same was  true in a sense because the politicians  that the matter would always had a  strong relationship with each other so  for example if a sheikh hamad would  supported a political candidate that  dramatically increased his opportunity  to win the election so it's really  interesting to see how this still  prevails in a way and I argue that this  event where the politicians listen to  the speeches of the Sheikh it's a way to  reaffirm this relationship and to show  that these two aspects of society and  that is religion and politics are still  very much intertwined  even though Senegal and The Gambia both  consider themselves to be secularized  countries who have a secular democracy  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  Bogle is a very special place to these  people and in ask sort of a sacredness  to it as well not only the Sheikh  himself but the village I was talking to  one of the followers of this group while  I was there and I asked about things  like like smoking or drinking alcohol  and and music and stuff and he said that  well a lot of people here are smokers  but no one smokes in this village if  they feel like they have to smoke then  they have to step outside the village  limits and smoke out