 unique and in contact  with some other source the divine in  this case God and these stories are also  corroborated by some of those ideals in  how he lives now that he lives somewhat  aesthetically that he's very humble he  isn't directly involved or interested in  politics he gives away a lot of charity  and all the money he receives he gives  to others and things like this he he  maintains that idea of a Sufi saint and  furthermore during the different rituals  and stuff he is always seated on a  heightened platform about  of everyone else also to symbolically  and aesthetically show that he is unique  that he is different from other people  another example of this is that he like  I mentioned early he doesn't talk  himself he always has someone beside him  that he whispers to and then this person  paraphrase is what he says for the  microphone and there's another way of  asserting his uniqueness to setting  apart from people and for him to to have  this role as a charismatic authoritative  figure that is a mediator between God  and the common folk or God and his  followers  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Applause]  there's a lot of charity happening in  this village as well not only does like  I said the Sheikh gave away a lot of his  gifts or all of his gifts according to  his followers but the community itself  sort of partakes in a lot of charities  so for example in the morning they would  often slaughter a cow and then cook it  and make a lot of food from it and  everyone in the compound would eat that  food but also the food would be given  away to those in the village who didn't  have the ability to cook their own food  or didn't have the money so there was  this constant charity of giving away  food and welcoming people to eat with us  that was also a very prominent and  important aspect of this pilgrimage  which also added to that sense of  community and that sense of of being  welcomed and I'm very very sort of  peaceful and really beautiful place to  be in in a lot of ways  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  for a lot of the people coming there it  is a way to reaffirm an identity and a  way to come together as a group of  people once there you'll be struck by  the incredible kindness and warmth of  the people and how welcomed you'll feel  I have never really experienced anything  like it it didn't matter that I was  Swedish or that I'm Christian or any of  those things  I was made to feel like I was part of  the group like I belong there and that's  something that I think we can all learn  from it was an incredible experience I  was talking to my friend site while I  was there and I asked him how he felt  about being there and being on this  pilgrimage and he said he felt really  good especially to experience but real  Islam is and I asked him what he meant  by that but is what is real Islam and he  just said we'll look around you everyone  takes care of each other everyone is  nice to each other there are thousands  of people in this village but there is  not a single fight and not a single  crime nothing like that it is a  community made up of love and peace and  that is to him what Islam is all about  that is real Islam this of course is in  great contrast to what a lot of people  especially here in the West think Islam  is I don't need to even describe the  kind of images that we get of Islam and  the opinions people have al Islam and  it's really striking to see the  difference in opinion of those people  who actually live that tradition and  those who don't or maybe don't have any  experience with it without making any  claims about what Islam is or isn't  because I can't do that a religion like  Islam is an incredibly diverse  traditions of all kinds of different  expressions but what I can say is that  what I experienced while I was in Bogle  and what I experienced when visiting the  group in Stockholm even is that very  Islam that my friend cite was describing  I'm a great believer in the value of  meeting people and getting to know  people from other cultures and people  you may not agree with for example  there's a lot of political turmoil in  Europe and  with America and all around the world  today with a lot of far-right ideologies  making their way into politics and stuff  and I think personally if you want to  try to work against bigotry and work  against racism and all these very foul  things that are creeping into society  again you know information isn't gonna  cut it  you know you can't talk to someone who  has a certain idea about Islam and just  give them information and they will  believe you because they have very  strong beliefs already the only thing  that's really gonna change someone's  mind is if they interact with other  people if a person who is afraid of  Muslims or have negative opinions about  Muslims actually get to know a Muslim  person or a group of Muslims and talk to  them there's a lot bigger chance that  they will change their mind and simply  by trying to espouse counter-arguments  and information on them by doing so  you'll notice that in most cases people  are nice and people want to integrate  into society they want to be part of  society they want to respect the laws of  the country they live in and if your  opinion is solely based on information  about for example Islam simply from the  news or from the media then of course  you're gonna have a negative opinion  about it it's like if all you knew about  airplanes was based on the news you'd  think that every airplane crashes but if  you actually get to know people if you  meet people if you talk to people you'll  notice that the reality of the situation  is a lot more complex and often in  opposition to what you see in the news  and therefore I urge everyone whether  you're an Islamophobe  or from a far-right political party in  Sweden or in Europe or whatever or even  if you're not one of those people I urge  you to go and meet these people to go  and talk to people even though like I  said I can't say that Islamist this or  that that Islam is either peaceful or  violent because Islam is both those  things any religion can lead to violence  and any religion can lead to peace but  what I've experienced in bogel and what  I've experienced growing up with Muslim  friends meeting different Muslim groups  and people of different cultures in  general is that in most of the cases  and especially in this case in boggle  what I experienced is love and kindness  and peace and community and a  willingness to coexist peacefully with  each other whether they be from the same  culture or from another culture from the  same religion or from another religion  that is the reality that I experienced  and experienced while I was on this  pilgrimage and if everyone can have just  the slice of that same experience then I  think this world might become a better  place eventually so thank you and I'll  see you next time  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you


